### Understanding the Hardware Serial Buffer

The ATmega328P microcontroller contains a dedicated hardware UART peripheral linked to a **64-byte circular FIFO (First-In, First-Out) buffer** in RAM.

```cpp
// Monster Lab - Hardware Serial Echo Diagnostic
void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600); // Initializes Pin 0 (RX) and Pin 1 (TX)
  while (!Serial) { ; }
  Serial.println("========================================");
  Serial.println("HARDWARE SERIAL BUFFER DIAGNOSTIC READY");
  Serial.println("Type characters into Serial Monitor and hit Send.");
  Serial.println("========================================");
}

void loop() {
  // Check how many bytes are currently waiting in the 64-byte ring buffer
  int bytesAvailable = Serial.available();

  if (bytesAvailable > 0) {
    Serial.print("Buffer contains ");
    Serial.print(bytesAvailable);
    Serial.print(" bytes. Echoing: '");

    // Read one byte out of the FIFO buffer
    char inChar = (char)Serial.read();
    Serial.print(inChar);
    Serial.println("'");
  }
}
```

### Code Breakdown:
1. `Serial.available()`: Returns the number of bytes queued in the 64-byte hardware FIFO buffer waiting to be read.
2. `Serial.read()`: Pulls the oldest byte from the buffer and frees up a slot.
3. **Ring Buffer Overflow Warning:** If 65 bytes arrive before `Serial.read()` is called, new incoming bytes are silently dropped!
